(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Attention Heads vs Attention Rollout", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"637d615d-34bb-9441-b8c6-d6be27bc915f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 ButtonBox["Vision Transformer example in the Neural Net Repository",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://resources.wolframcloud.com/NeuralNetRepository/resources/\
Vision-Transformer-Trained-on-ImageNet-Competition-Data/"], None}],
 " extracts attention from the last transformer block, shows the heads \
separately, and overlays their mean on the input image. It is easy to try, \
and the pictures invite a natural interpretation of the bright regions being \
where the model looked at."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  4.004451642747366*^9, {4.0044540921949043`*^9, 
   4.004454101401716*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"32bd7d65-30ff-2f46-918a-\
8de2e4183df8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "There is however a complication: the last layer reads representations that \
have already passed through many rounds of mixing. Samira Abnar and Willem \
Zuidema address this in ",
 ButtonBox["Quantifying Attention Flow in Transformers (2020)",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.385/"], None}],
 ", introducing attention rollout and attention flow. Rollout combines \
attention across layers to estimate connections back to the original inputs. \
That sounded like a useful extension to the Wolfram example, so I wanted to \
compare the two."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0044516509648113`*^9, 
  4.004451652073345*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"de6c8325-ac4d-4b4d-880f-\
701d8851c161"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A familiar animal example", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"24ffa0cf-e3ac-3041-bc61-bdb91c5ec618"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "I used the white Samoyed from ",
 ButtonBox["PyTorch Hub's image-classification example",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://pytorch.org/hub/pytorch_vision_resnet/"], None}],
 ". A single dog image makes the comparison easier to follow than a scene \
containing several animals, plus I love this example. Here the model being \
used is Wolfram's ViT Base/16, with twelve blocks and twelve heads per block."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004452620920273*^9, 4.0044526330611725`*^9}, {
  4.0044548930136166`*^9, 
  4.0044548996494026`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4729b048-507d-3047-a31b-\
f31d23a02333"],

Cell["\<\
The actual model crop is 224 by 224 pixels. It becomes a 14 by 14 grid of \
patch tokens, plus the classification token, CLS. The model then predicts \
Samoyed with 75.2% probability. Below, the left map is the mean of the final \
block's CLS-to-patch attention, while the right is standard mean-head rollout \
through all twelve blocks.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004451752797882*^9, 
  4.0044517598399544`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"36e82d91-105d-ec4a-a1bf-\
ceb0d0967751"],

