Cell["\<\
Same model crop, color map and opacity. Contrast is adjusted separately in \
the two panels; brightness does not compare absolute attention weights.\
\>", "Caption",ExpressionUUID->"12131c5a-d3ef-9b4a-a379-f121a15b287b"],

Cell["\<\
I expected rollout to produce a cleaner outline of the dog, but the result is \
a bit less tidy. The head mean has several sharp peaks in the grass and \
foliage. Rollout spreads more broadly over the dog's head, body and tail, but \
retains strong background peaks near the top. It is hard to say with only \
this example if rollout is truly better for visualizing attention.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0044517713523293`*^9, 4.0044517949949207`*^9}, {
  4.0044518257148895`*^9, 
  4.0044518719023952`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ba51e655-bfa7-8644-8964-\
cf95f513630a"],

Cell["\<\
Here is a second Samoyed photo, this time we get a side view of the dog. With \
the same model and settings. Samoyed is again the top prediction, at 85.0%. \
The head mean has sharp spots on the dog and on the floor, while rollout is \
giving a broader highlight around the neck, back and the curled tail. The \
difference in concentration is visible again, although neither map neatly \
outlines the animal.\
