Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Individual heads and layers", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004451935959137*^9, 
  4.004451939154196*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8c0578d0-f8f5-9444-9534-\
6447423cdf7a"],

Cell["\<\
Returning to the original PyTorch photograph, the mean also hides differences \
between heads. In the final block, head 8 is the most concentrated, while \
head 12 is the most spread out. Head 8 emphasizes a few background patches, \
head 12 includes the dog's face and tail more visibly. So picking the \
sharpest head would not automatically give the most recognizable animal \
localization. These are also observations about this image, and not permanent \
jobs assigned to those heads.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0044527833263245`*^9, 
  4.0044528468395977`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6a56ca2a-4a15-2c42-8a25-\
779646c4fd77"],

Cell["\<\
The plot below follows all twelve layers. The shaded band spans the \
individual heads, the blue line describes their mean map, and the orange line \
describes cumulative rollout. To compare concentration, I normalize the \
spatial weights and calculate entropy, divided by the maximum possible \
entropy. A value of 1 means uniform weights over the patches, while lower \
values mean greater concentration.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004451957501768*^9, 
  4.004451958638485*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c2142607-b6da-fe4d-b364-\
0cb10bd7ece0"],

