Cell["\<\
The band is the range of individual head entropies. The blue line is the \
entropy of the averaged map, not the average of those entropies. Entropy here \
is measuring the distribution shape.\
\>", "Caption",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004451970024826*^9, 
  4.004451993741577*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6be769bc-4d57-ad47-831d-\
2e6a0e6a1102"],

Cell["\<\
At the final layer, the mean-head map has entropy 0.753, compared with 0.984 \
for rollout. Individual heads range from 0.616 to 0.875. The later raw maps \
become more concentrated, while rollout stays close to uniform. It does not \
become progressively more diffuse at every step, separate contrast adjustment \
can make small differences in a nearly uniform map look quite dramatic.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004452002608164*^9, 
  4.0044520460164185`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"aacc354e-2d36-8a49-a9a8-\
a725dc5a926e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A snowy scene and a Great Pyrenees prediction", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004452869345028*^9, 
  4.0044528716209087`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c1f20a1b-4c93-2f45-9fb3-\
2e99346e31d3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The winter photograph below adds a more interesting comparison. Its ",
 ButtonBox["source identifies the dog as a Samoyed",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samoyed_winter.jpg"], None}],
 ", but the model predicts Great Pyrenees with 83.0% probability, Samoyed \
comes second at 10.1%. The dog in this case is much smaller in the 224 by 224 \
crop, and is surrounded by snow and trees."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004452888795967*^9, 4.0044529551208344`*^9}, {
  4.004454166541216*^9, 
  4.004454168209837*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f6370663-2a45-9743-ae37-\
867becbae5ae"],

