  "samoyed-option-5-post-001/comparison.png",ExpressionUUID->"180fa60f-27ea-\
3e42-944a-e80465cc8ade"],

Cell["", "Caption",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0044515372803383`*^9, 
  4.004451539310131*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"88e2e2d2-a836-e042-81bc-\
66d67b3d17b1"],

Cell["\<\
The raw map highlights the dog, but it also has strong peaks on the snow and \
trees. Its largest patch weight is on a tree in the upper-right background, \
and the second largest is on the dog. Rollout spreads more broadly across the \
scene, including the snowy foreground. The breed confusion and the background \
highlights together suggest a useful hypothesis: scene context may have \
helped push the prediction toward Great Pyrenees.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0044529692767086`*^9, 
  4.004452969810425*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"32d7e097-d1ec-4943-9f08-\
34b23422254f"],

Cell["\<\
There are two reasons to keep that as a hypothesis. First, these maps do not \
use a particular class score, so they cannot tell us which regions favor \
Great Pyrenees over Samoyed. A late token positioned over snow may also carry \
information gathered from the dog. Second, rollout is nearly uniform here: \
its normalized spatial entropy is 0.994. Even its strongest patch carries \
only 1.20% of the spatial weight, compared with 0.51% for a uniform map. \
Stretching the contrast makes those differences look stronger.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fddb6eac-5b90-7545-8519-c96c03fc3007"],

Cell["\<\
For me, this makes the winter image a useful diagnostic case. A follow-up \
could keep the dog's size and position fixed, change or obscure the \
background in several ways, and track the Great Pyrenees and Samoyed scores. \
A consistent shift would strengthen the background-influence interpretation. \
These interventions have not been run here, the current observation is the \
breed error together with the attention pattern.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004452099806631*^9, 
  4.00445210020319*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a2acfc67-f596-b744-9091-\
5849237e5f8c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["How rollout is calculated", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"5513ee3b-377b-6949-81e1-cf898d4223c3"],

Cell["\<\
To clarify the rollout calculation, for each layer we\[CloseCurlyQuote]re \
essentially averaging the attention matrices across heads. Add the identity \
matrix to include a residual route, then normalize every row. Starting from \
the identity, one multiplies each new layer on the left of the accumulated \
result. In Wolfram Language, with tensor axes {layer, head, query, key}, the \
core calculation is:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0044521256852036`*^9, 
  4.004452167958082*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"295f5545-8af1-564e-acd0-\
506a54a92bb3"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"attentionRollout", "[", "attention_", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"meanHeads", ",", "n", ",", "transitions"}], "}"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"meanHeads", "=", 
      RowBox[{"Mean", "/@", "attention"}]}], ";", 
     RowBox[{"n", "=", 
      RowBox[{"Length", "[", 
       RowBox[{"First", "[", "meanHeads", "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
     RowBox[{"transitions", "=", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"(", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"#1", "+", 
          RowBox[{"IdentityMatrix", "[", "n", "]"}]}], "&"}], ")"}], "/@", 
       "meanHeads"}]}], ";", 
     RowBox[{"transitions", "=", 
      RowBox[{"Map", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         FractionBox["#1", 
          RowBox[{"Total", "[", "#1", "]"}]], "&"}], ",", "transitions", ",", 
        RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ";", 
     RowBox[{"Fold", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"#2", ".", "#1"}], "&"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"IdentityMatrix", "[", "n", "]"}], ",", "transitions"}], 
      "]"}]}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b878644f-838a-6141-87d8-\
038d263a4861"],

Cell["\<\
The multiplication order follows the route from the original tokens through \
the successive layers. CLS stays in every matrix product. Only afterwards do \
we take its row, discard the CLS column, and reshape the remaining 196 \
weights into a 14 by 14 map. Removing CLS earlier would remove routes that \
pass through it.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"79a05e3d-ad77-f849-87d0-04f1ca51b31f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Adding the identity gives the attention and residual routes equal weight \
after normalization. This is a modeling choice used in ",
 ButtonBox["the authors' rollout implementation",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://github.com/samiraabnar/attention_flow/blob/master/attention_\
graph_util.py"], None}],
 "; it is not a complete simulation of a transformer block. Learned value and \
output projections, normalization and MLPs are outside this calculation."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c197e3f9-ab56-cb47-ae15-7d9c92f077cf"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A small calculation makes the distinction clearer", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"fe19752b-274b-0f4d-a39f-ae68794e8d70"],

Cell["\<\
Consider three tokens in the order {CLS, A, B}, with scalar values {0, 2, \
10}. Hold the attention matrices fixed and use identity value projections. \
First let A copy B. In the next layer, let CLS copy A. For this first \
calculation, omit residuals and other transformations. The values are:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"191bd8ba-e1de-114c-8774-f57a8bbddd13"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {"\<\"Token\"\>", "\<\"Input\"\>", "\<\"After layer 1\"\>", "\<\"After \
layer 2\"\>"},
    {"\<\"CLS\"\>", "0", "0", "10"},
    {"\<\"A\"\>", "2", "10", "10"},
    {"\<\"B\"\>", "10", "10", "10"}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Center}}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{0.8}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"6bbe7222-2b9b-bc4c-ba40-5c54bb8f33c9"],

Cell["\<\
The final attention row is {0, 1, 0}: CLS reads intermediate A. The product \
of both attention matrices has first row {0, 0, 1}: the value came from \
original B. Both statements are correct. The mistake would be treating \
intermediate A as if it were still an untouched input token.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"344851df-c138-4d43-8196-fddc2e3c2974"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"x0", "=", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", "2", ",", "10"}], "}"}]}], ";", RowBox[{"a1", "=", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "0", ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", "0", ",", "1"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", "0", ",", "1"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ";", RowBox[{"a2", "=", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", "1", ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", "1", ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", "0", ",", "1"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ";", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"First", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"First", "[", RowBox[{"a2", ".", "a1"}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"First", "[", RowBox[{"a2", ".", "a1", ".", "x0"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}]], "Input",\
ExpressionUUID->"34ef3454-8471-6342-8463-94b6da1385b4"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"0", ",", "1", ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"0", ",", "0", ",", "1"}], "}"}], ",", "10"}], "}"}]], "Output",Ex\
pressionUUID->"e3538242-9148-4f40-8a9f-5481a2bd120b"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
We can also add equal residual mixing explicitly, replacing each matrix a by \
(a + I)/2. The final input coefficients become {1/2, 1/4, 1/4}, and CLS \
becomes 3. A and B now have equal routing coefficients, but their scalar \
contributions are 0.5 and 2.5 because their input values differ. Even in this \
simple, exact example, a routing weight and a value contribution are \
different quantities.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"973c9df8-0503-6f4b-a1f5-331077eb0598"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Conclusions", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0044524481806717`*^9, 
  4.004452450083088*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8ba0e165-09f4-3e48-b510-\
810c78ea40e1"],

Cell["\<\
The real model is a more complicated black-box, but the distinction we found \
still helps us read the maps. Final-layer attention selects intermediate \
token representations and rollout estimates their earlier input routes. In \
the original PyTorch example, those estimated histories have already become \
very similar across tokens by layer 11, so the final layer mostly combines \
similar, broad distributions. That explains why its sharp raw peaks do not \
turn into sharp rollout peaks.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0044522278969917`*^9, 4.004452229481806*^9}, {
  4.0044524139826546`*^9, 
  4.004452430615675*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"425e139c-c044-1b43-82b6-\
998b77d79a4d"],

Cell["\<\
I would keep rollout as an additional view alongside the existing \
attention-head visualization. It answers a useful question about paths across \
layers. These examples also give us specific behavior to investigate: \
concentrated background peaks, diffuse rollout, and a breed error in the \
snowy scene. Explaining the Samoyed or Great Pyrenees score specifically \
would require a class-sensitive method or an intervention test. The \
comparison helps us formulate those questions and understand why the maps \
differ.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.004453044518242*^9, 
  4.0044530459611073`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a7636c84-5a5d-e442-acaa-\
d7a78482563c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The native Wolfram calculation uses upstream standard normalization with \
unchanged model weights. The cached image, tensors and reproducibility \
details are recorded in README.md. ",
 ButtonBox["Pinned image source",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://github.com/pytorch/hub/blob/\
c3beaae7d32fca2a23fec30aa7938ef5c9b6e5d5/images/dog.jpg"], None}],
 "."
}], "Caption",ExpressionUUID->"975f9342-d0f1-ef4f-a6a6-716b79370012"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["One more Samoyed", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"53f1e0a6-eef0-0f45-8d10-1bba927a2a55"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To finish, here is ",
 ButtonBox["another Samoyed on grass",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samoyed_dog.jpg"], None}],
 ". The model predicts Samoyed with 90.6% probability. The dog fills much \
more of the crop than in the winter scene, yet the head mean still has some \
background peaks again. Rollout on the other hand is more diffuse and \
includes parts of the face and body, so a correct breed prediction can \
coexist with an attention map that does not simply trace the dog."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0044524747214756`*^9, 4.0044525043694935`*^9}, {
  4.0044547059458847`*^9, 
  4.0044547421508617`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5a299a62-60e7-5d4f-a82a-\
7e688370287b"],

